## `get_cpu_idle_time_us` — the full body

```c
u64 get_cpu_idle_time_us(int cpu, u64 *last_update_time)
{
	struct tick_sched *ts = &per_cpu(tick_cpu_sched, cpu);
	ktime_t now, idle;

	if (!tick_nohz_active)
		return -1;

	now = ktime_get();
	if (last_update_time) {
		update_ts_time_stats(cpu, ts, now, last_update_time);
		idle = ts->idle_sleeptime;
	} else {
		if (ts->idle_active && !nr_iowait_cpu(cpu)) {
			ktime_t delta = ktime_sub(now, ts->idle_entrytime);

			idle = ktime_add(ts->idle_sleeptime, delta);
		} else {
			idle = ts->idle_sleeptime;
		}
	}

	return ktime_to_us(idle);
}
```

### 1) Is `cpu_online()` checked?

No. The only early-out is the NOHZ enablement flag:

> `if (!tick_nohz_active)`
> `return -1;`

The function is otherwise unguarded — it takes `&per_cpu(tick_cpu_sched, cpu)` and reads the per-CPU fields directly, without checking whether that CPU is online. In contrast, code paths that stop the tick do include that guard, e.g. `can_stop_idle_tick`:

> `if (unlikely(!cpu_online(cpu)))`

### 2) How `idle_entrytime` / `idle_sleeptime` are treated

- With a non-NULL `last_update_time`, `update_ts_time_stats()` folds the elapsed interval into `idle_sleeptime` (or `iowait_sleeptime` if `nr_iowait_cpu(cpu) > 0`) and moves `idle_entrytime` up to `now`.
- With a NULL pointer, nothing is mutated; the code only adds a live delta when the CPU is currently marked idle and not in iowait:
> `idle = ktime_add(ts->idle_sleeptime, delta);`

So on an offline CPU whose `idle_active` still read as 1, a NULL-pointer call would keep adding `now - idle_entrytime` — the counters are never frozen for offline CPUs by this function itself.

### 3) Reset vs. continued growth

Growth is stopped elsewhere, at tick teardown. `tick_cancel_sched_timer()` cancels the emulation timer and then wipes the whole per-CPU structure:

```c
	if (ts->sched_timer.base)
		hrtimer_cancel(&ts->sched_timer);
#endif

	memset(ts, 0, sizeof(*ts));
```

Because of that blanket `memset(ts, 0, sizeof(*ts))`, `idle_sleeptime`, `idle_entrytime`, `idle_active`, `idle_jiffies` and the rest are zeroed — i.e. the accumulated idle value is **reset, not carried forward**. Consequently an offline CPU's idle time does not keep climbing after teardown; it either reports the last values if teardown hasn't happened yet, or zero afterwards. Note the reset also destroys history: total idle since boot is lost across an offline/online cycle rather than being preserved and resumed. Also, `idle_active` being zeroed means the `ktime_add` branch above cannot fire post-teardown.

One caveat: the `-1` return only reflects `!tick_nohz_active`, not CPU state, so callers cannot distinguish "offline" from "tickless disabled" via the return value alone.

